                <pb facs="0374" n="1334" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1334"/>

                <head>No. 895.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Roosevelt to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The
                                Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1888-10-20"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 20, 1888</date>. (Received
                        October 30.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 37.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reference to the case of Mr. Hector, who
                    complained of the refusal of the railways to carry hog products which came from
                    America, mentioned in my dispatch No. 23, of September 28, I have reason to
                    believe that he had not complied with the requisite forms, and having so
                    informed him, have heard nothing further in the matter, and shall do nothing
                    further at present.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. B. Roosevelt.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
